[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Thank you to Insta360 for sponsoring this video. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; All right, hope you enjoyed that travel'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'film. I shot all of it on the new Insta360 X6 360 camera. The main settings I used were 8K iLog and slow-motion mode for a few slow-mo shots. And in this video, I\'ll be giving my review after having traveled with it'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for over a month. This isn\'t going to be a review of all the new features because honestly, a lot of them, such as the AI director and editing tools, are more for beginners, so they\'re not really features I would use. But before we get into my specific thoughts, I will go'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'through the overall checklist of what\'s new in the X6. Here\'s what I consider the most important features right up front in bold. The X6 now has dual 1.1 in sensors. It shoots 8K slow motion, and'

[02:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it records 10-bit color. For me, these features are what matters, and everything else is just details. But we\'ll keep going anyway. All right, about those sensors. The X6 uses dual 1.1 in Sony sensors with a'

[02:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'triple AI system and Insta360 claims that that means better low-light performance, less noise, and 14.5 stops of dynamic range. They claim 300% more light intake per pixel versus the X5,'

[02:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and 35% lower power use. The X6 has three major color modes, standard, Dolby Vision for HDR, and at long last, 10-bit iLog with ACES support. So, now you can grade your X6 footage just like all the'

[03:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'other high-end cameras out there. And you do get color recovery while you record, but I couldn\'t seem to get the LUT to work on playback in camera. I only got it to work using the Insta360 app. It\'s waterproof up to 20 m, and with a'

[03:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dive case, it can go to 60 m. The screen is different now. It\'s a 2.32 in OLED, which is bigger and wider than the previous X cameras. In my experience, it\'s definitely a lot easier'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to get an idea of your framing with this screen. You can enable a feature called full-screen press, which means that you can press anywhere on the screen to start and stop recording, which is helpful if you\'re wearing gloves. And you don\'t need to worry about'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'accidentally triggering the recording very easily because it requires a hard sustained press. You also still get voice control, gesture control, and there\'s a new selfie stick feature for starting and stopping recording or snapping photos. I never used any of these features.'

[03:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The lenses are still replaceable, and now the replacement lenses are cheaper to buy in a more portable case. They are more scratch resistant and they\'re easier to assemble. However, I never had to replace a lens during my shoot, so my backup lenses are still sitting in their'

[04:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'case. Battery life is supposed to be 140 minutes of continuous recording, which sounds about right for my experience, and there\'s 47 GB of built-in storage. [music] Now, it is great having that internal storage, but I used up the entire 47 GB in less than a day of heavy'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shooting. Practically speaking, you\'ll still need an SD [music] card. For audio, it has a new hidden engine mic that\'s supposed to improve the clarity for recording MotoVlogs, but I can\'t verify that because I almost never use'

[04:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the internal audio at all. You can also connect the Mic Pro and the Mic Air directly, no receiver required. Okay, that\'s the high-level overview of what\'s new. Now, let\'s get into how I use the X6 to make a travel film. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[04:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Whenever I post these reviews, I get a lot of questions about camera settings and almost none about the actual locations and subjects that I\'m filming. But, where you film, what you film, and the lighting you film in are the absolute most important parts of getting a good image. For my 360 videos, I look'

[05:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for environments that surround me, like this beach with its dramatic horseshoe-shaped cove. No matter what direction you look here, there\'s something amazing to see. But, it doesn\'t have to be a huge space. This small crack between these rocks full of'

[05:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shellfish made for a great subject because the walls of this little mini canyon surrounded the camera as I moved through them. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Add a little vortex spin, and you get a pretty cool shot. Similarly, this narrow street was great'

[05:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for doing a high-angle follow shot of a moped because you can feel the motion as we move through the confined space. Or, you can start close up and then move away, like I did with this fishing net. [music] One way or another, I just always try to surround the camera with'

[05:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'some kind of interesting environment. What doesn\'t work well for 360 is far away subjects. Here we\'ve got this beautiful stone city surrounded by a lot of cars in the foreground. In the 360 shot, those cars become the focus of the'

[05:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scene and you can\'t crop them out without seriously degrading the image quality. When you\'re shooting 360, everything is in ultra sharp focus. Every other tourist, every sign, everything. So you have to choose environments where'

[06:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'no matter how much you show, it still looks good. And lighting is especially important, not just for the look, but because of the dreaded selfie stick shadow. The software can erase the stick, but so far we can\'t get rid of that shadow.'

[06:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So I try to shoot when there are no shadows, like before sunrise, or I film in the shade, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; or I film in a busy environment where there\'s so much going on that you won\'t even notice the shadow in the shot, or me.'

[06:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now I\'m going to talk about a few of my favorite scenes starting with this [music] fisherman. I filmed him because he works on one of the last traditional fishing nets in Puglia that\'s still in operation. We got up at sunrise to film the daily'

[06:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'operations, which provided a lot of opportunities for me to do dramatic crane movements across the fishing platform. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Notice how I was always filming in backlight to avoid getting the selfie stick shadow in the shot. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[06:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Thanks to the high dynamic range of the X6 in log mode, I still had detail in the fisherman\'s face [music] and in the sky behind him. But also the fishing platform was still reflecting sun back in his face, acting as sort of a natural bounce card. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[07:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; For angles looking out beyond the platform, I just stuck the selfie stick through the wood slats and I got a perspective that you wouldn\'t get with any other type of camera. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; For the scooter scene, I made some new friends in the beautiful town called'

[07:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Conversano. I rode on the back of a scooter holding the extended selfie stick following the other scooter, which gave me this overhead perspective. Then I rode in front of them and I got these shots.'

[07:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Reframing in post allowed me to add perfectly framed camera movements. Then I let my friends film themselves on the scooter by giving the selfie stick'

[07:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to the woman in the back. And here\'s what they filmed of themselves. Not bad. Later in the trip in Matera, we went for a ride in open-top car and I extended [music] the selfie stick above to get'

[07:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'these drone-like shots. By moving the selfie stick, I could make the camera orbit us. For a back view, I just rested the stick on my shoulder and this is the perspective that I got.'

[08:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It was a lot of fun playing around with the different angles, but of course, if you\'re going to do this, make sure there\'s no other objects your selfie stick could collide with on the road. We got up at 4:00 a.m. to take this'

[08:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'traditional boat around Polignano a Mare. By mid-morning, this peaceful scene would become a chaotic mess of boats and kayaks, but at sunrise, we had it all to ourselves. A 360 camera is great for filming the entire boat all at once, plus your surroundings.'

[08:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; And since the X6 is waterproof, I held it near the water and filmed this dramatic angle in slow motion. Polignano is famous for its sea caves. These caves have extremely high contrast, so even with the great dynamic range of the X6,'

[08:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I had to choose whether to expose for the inside of the cave or outside. For instance, for this shot, I exposed for the inside. Notice that the entrance is completely overexposed. And then for this one, I chose to expose'

[09:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for the sky to show the birds flying away. And here\'s another angle where I exposed for the exterior. Later in the day, I found people cliff jumping into the sea. So, I brought out'

[09:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the X6 and I used my extended selfie stick again. Sometimes I made the camera fall with them. Sometimes I flew the camera over them. And for this jump, I put the camera'

[09:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'under the guy as he jumped, and then I reframed in post to tilt down and follow him. I shot all of this in 48p, which makes for perfect 50% slow motion in a 24p timeline. For some of the jumps, I'

[09:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reframed the field of view to zoom out in post, making the dive look even more dramatic. So, here\'s the dive without the zoom. And now here it is with the zoom. [music] There\'s a town called Alberobello, which'

[10:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is famous for these cone-shaped houses called trulli. They used to be sort of an undiscovered gem, but [music] not anymore. Which is why you don\'t see much of this town in the short film. I wanted to film a shot of myself walking out of a trulli home, so [music]'

[10:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I extended the selfie stick partway because the interior of the home was pretty small. Then, I walked with the stick in front of me at waist level, and I continued into the street. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[10:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; In post, I reframed it to create a 180° pan to myself, revealing the home behind me. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; For another shot, I stood as far as possible across the street, and then I walked forward while I lowered the selfie stick, [music] and I aimed it toward the door.'

[10:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This gave me a flying shot toward the home and going into the dark doorway, which could be perfect for a scene transition. When I was wandering around the town of'

[10:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Vieste at dusk, I found these rocks by the sea, so I decided to go for a little jog. I got that follow shot just by holding the stick over my shoulder. And by changing the angle of the selfie stick, I could get different angles of myself as I ran.'

[11:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Then I put the selfie stick in a hole with one of the lenses facing up, and I jumped over it. In slow motion, this became a dramatic flying leap.'

[11:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The way I like to do a hyperlapse is to extend the selfie stick all the way, and then run like a weirdo, holding the stick in front of me, not directly above me, to ensure that my body doesn\'t end'

[11:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'up in the frame. I also try to pass underneath objects if possible to give more of a feeling of motion. This hyperlapse is only 3x speed because I wanted to keep the feeling of natural motion in the hanging bed sheets. For this low light hyperlapse in Conversano,'

[11:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I set my shutter speed slower &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; to 1/50 of a second, and I just very slowly walked forward as steadily as possible. I passed by some people, but I figured it would be okay because the'

[11:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'camera was going to be pointing upward in the reframing. And here\'s the result sped up 20 times. When I visited the abandoned town Craco, there was one thing I really wanted to'

[12:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shoot, the abandoned church, but it was off limits due to safety concerns. But I was still able to get a shot because I could slide my selfie stick through the fence, [music] and then I just did a little camera move, and I got this beautiful shot.'

[12:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; There\'s a festival called Mercantia in the town of Certaldo Alto, and my friend Marco got me special permission to film [music] it with the X6. However, I\'m'

[12:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'aware that the big selfie stick is pretty annoying to other people, so I waited for specific moments to film. [music] Then I raised up the stick, did some slow movements over the crowd like a crane, then once I had enough footage, I'

[12:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dropped the selfie stick and I retracted [music] it. There was also this dancer in a dark room, and honestly I thought it would be too dark to film in there, but with a 1/50 shutter and the base ISO of 100, I still got a great exposure. Once again,'

[12:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I moved slowly to avoid creating motion blur with the camera. Finally, we got permission to climb up a tower to film these hanging dancers. Because we got the special permission to film from above, I was able to get an'

[13:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'incredible view looking straight down on the show. So thanks to my friend Marco and the low light capabilities of the X6, I could capture Mercantia in all its glory. Well, now you see my footage from every'

[13:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'situation where I\'ve used the X6 so far, and the question is, should you buy it? Well, here\'s my honest opinion. It is an incremental upgrade, but it\'s an important one for pro users. You\'re not going to get more resolution than the X5, but you are getting a bigger sensor'

[13:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and better low-light performance. And it shoots 10-bit log and 8K slow motion. And grading that 10-bit log footage was such a pleasure for me compared to 8-bit on the previous Insta360 [music]'

[13:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cameras. The camera\'s functionality otherwise is basically the same as before, so it doesn\'t change what a 360 camera can and cannot do. So really, that just leaves one question. Do you like the way the X6 looks? If so, I say go for it. If not,'

[13:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'then maybe this isn\'t the upgrade for you. And remember, the X5 and the X4 Air are still out there depending on your needs and depending on your budget. Okay, so that\'s it for me. Please check the gear links in the description. Thank you so much for watching and I\'ll see'

[14:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you next time.'
